- trap: the mistake an exam question could test that the slide's own content invites — a reversed inequality, the inverse instead of the matrix, the letter or index that changes, a sum for a product, the condition dropped — at most 60 characters; "" when there is none. It comes from the slides, never from sample exam questions.
- refs: the home ref first (role home: the page that states the formula), then at most 3 also refs (pages that use it again).
- en: name (at most 70 characters), note (at most 180) and trap (at most 140) in concise exam English with the slides' own terms, keeping every symbol and condition; trap and en.trap are both filled or both empty; no Chinese characters in en.
- Cover every formula an exam could ask about: definitions, estimators, criteria, update rules, model equations, objectives and constraints, rules of thumb with a number. Leave out notation reminders that carry no content.

Report only real problems, most severe first; not style. Each finding: severity — must when it is wrong or misleading for the exam, should when it is clearly worse than it could be, nit when it is small; where — the ids it is about (nodes, contrasts or formulas; a link's two ends; none when something is missing or it is about the tour or the summary); field — the field at fault ("" for the whole item); the problem; evidence — each page that shows it, with a verbatim quote of its text, or for a picture of its reading (code drops a finding without one, or whose quote is not there); fix — the exact replacement text, or the full new item. An empty list when nothing survives your own checking.",
    "You are a sceptical checker of findings about a course's formula sheet. Try to refute each finding by reading the cited slide pages yourself. A finding about a text is real only if the current text is actually wrong or clearly misleading per the slides — not merely improvable — and its proposed fix is itself correct per the slides and keeps the rules. A finding that something is missing is real when the cited pages teach it, the sheet has it nowhere under any name, and the proposed item is itself correct per those pages and keeps the rules. When unsure, it is not real. Give your reason in one or two sentences.

